:null},{"season":2018,"team":"CHI","distance":0.704,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Broncos went 7-9 in 2019 and were outscored 316 to 282. The defense did most of the work, finishing 16th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 24th in EPA per play. What set them apart was the 3rd highest I formation usage and the highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","offense":"The Broncos offense was well below average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 3rd highest I formation usage in the league. Joe Flacco ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Courtland Sutton, who drew 26% of the targets.","defense":"The Broncos defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 12th, and its weakest was pressure rate, where it ranked 31st. Von Miller was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Derek Wolfe, who had 7 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. Schematically, they had the highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","special":"The Broncos were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 15% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 85% of field goals, which was a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 25% of the money on the books. Their tight ends were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Joe Flacco played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 1,822 yards. Courtland Sutton was the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 1,112 receiving yards. Phillip Lindsay handled most of the work in the backfield with 61% of the carries. On defense, Von Miller led the team with 8 sacks and Justin Simmons picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2022 Texans and the 2019 Lions. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Chargers under Brandon Staley and the 2019 Bears.","trends":"The offense went from 21st in EPA per play in 2018 to 24th in 2019, a modest decline. The defense moved from 16th in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 16th in 2019, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was number of defenders in the box, which went from 6.56 to 6.08.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}